{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve traveled to lots of weird places on this show - from the interiors of black holes to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4400,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the time before the big bang. But today I want to take you on a journey that has got to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9120,
    "dDurationMs": 6035,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weirdest place in the modern universe  - a place where matter exists in states  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15155,
    "dDurationMs": 5165,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that I bet you’ve never heard of. Today we take a journey to the center of the neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25360,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutron stars are arguably the strangest objects in the universe - if we don’t count black holes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as actual objects. And honestly, neutron stars are even weirder than black holes in some ways.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve talked about these things before - about how they form from the dead cores of massive stars,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41920,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "left over after supernova explosions. We’ve \nseen how they look from afar - perfect  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47840,
    "dDurationMs": 6536,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "metronomes of flashing light as these rapidly spinning stars sweep us with their jets as pulsars. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54376,
    "dDurationMs": 8157,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we’ve explored strange processes that occur as a neutron star approaches becoming a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62533,
    "dDurationMs": 7078,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what we’ve never done - at least not properly - is to really explore the insides of these beasts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69611,
    "dDurationMs": 5829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’s time we did so, because there we’ll find states of matter that exist nowhere else in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75440,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe. For this journey we’re going \nto need an unthinkably advanced spacecraft.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, there is no imaginable \ntechnology that could withstand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the conditions that we’re about to experience - so we’re relying on the amazing power  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91120,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of bad science fiction. An indestructium \ncraft to withstand the radiation and pressure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and an anti-gravitational field to resist \nthe ridiculous gravitational forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And all this is going to come in handy as soon as we approach the neutron star. The first thing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we encounter is its magnetosphere. This is the strongest magnetic field in the universe. Even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weakest neutron star fields are a billion \ntimes stronger than those of the earth or sun."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120720,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This magnetosphere is very different from \nthe magnetized space around the earth or sun.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125760,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s filled with electrons and \npositrons. These matter-antimatter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 129600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pairs are created out of the extreme \nenergy photons in the magnetic field.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That field then becomes a particle accelerator, with electron currents flowing one way and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "positron currents flowing the other way- due to their opposite electric charges. These and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145600,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other charged particles end up being blasted out along the poles of the magnetic field,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and their motion in those jets results in \nthe radiation that we observe as pulsars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155360,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we’re nearly through the magnetosphere we start to notice that the neutron star’s surface  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a little fuzzy. We’re seeing the star’s \natmosphere. Similar to Earth’s atmosphere,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165920,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this layer of haze starts out \nvery tenuous - almost a vacuum,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170000,
    "dDurationMs": 4876,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then gets denser as we drop. \nBut the similarities end there.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174876,
    "dDurationMs": 5604,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s atmosphere is mostly oxygen and nitrogen in molecular form. Pressure increases as you go  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180480,
    "dDurationMs": 6853,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down, so that at Earth’s surface the weight of all that gas on top of your head is about 200kg."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187333,
    "dDurationMs": 5307,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the neutron star’s atmosphere \nis not made of atoms, rather it's a plasma,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192640,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which atoms have been stripped of \ntheir electrons, or ionized,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to the extreme heat - around a million \nKelvin for a young neutron star. Those nuclei  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are mostly hydrogen and helium, captured from the nearly-empty space surrounding the star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while Earth’s atmosphere is something like 100 km thick, depending on how you define the edge of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space, the neutron star’s atmosphere is barely a meter thick, with most of the plasma confined to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218800,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a thin shell 10cm above the star’s surface. This is due to the insane gravity at that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface - which I’ll come back to. Once we’ve landed on the surface, our heads are in the vacuum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232160,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space, while our feet are in a foggy plasma with a density many million times greater  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238480,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then anything on Earth. And the gravitational pull is something like a 100 billion G's."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 244960,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here we start to encounter the first truly strange states of matter. See, the matter at your feet is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252320,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not all that different from the stuff inside \nof a white dwarf - the dead core of a lower  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass star like our Sun. The plasma is crushed so tight that electrons are on the verge of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overlapping. But as we saw in previous episodes, particles of the fermion family can’t occupy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268880,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same quantum state. The matter has \nbecome what we call degenerate, and electron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273920,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "degeneracy pressure stops further collapse and ultimately holds the atmosphere up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Having landed on the neutron star, we \nactually do have a solid surface below our feet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It appears to be a strange sort of \ncrystalline material. Now that’s pretty  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weird because we normally think of crystals as lattices of atoms connected by electron bonds.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the stuff below our feet is still completely ionized - stripped of its electrons. In fact it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303680,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a frozen plasma, in which its nuclei are locked together in a regular lattice. You might wonder  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310480,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how nuclei can bind to each other given that they’re all positively charged and so should  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315600,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repel. Well, at these densities nuclei are \npushed so close together that their mutual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321760,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "repulsion actually prevents nuclei from slipping past each other, like gridlocked traffic. And the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "symmetry of that repulsion forces nuclei into a regular grid. In this case the crystalline  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334720,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter is mostly iron. That iron was the last \nelement forged in the core of the star in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341440,
    "dDurationMs": 6852,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hours before it went supernova - and some of it still survives here at the neutron star surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348292,
    "dDurationMs": 5121,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now the real journey can begin as we \nstart to tunnel into the star’s interior.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353413,
    "dDurationMs": 5787,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We enter the outer crust of the star. Density only increases as we go down. Suffusing the crystal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lattice is a gas of electrons - a so-called \ndegenerate fermi gas that holds up this part of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365280,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the star from further collapse. The deeper we go, the more energetic these electrons become - and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371120,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "soon those energies are high enough to drive some very exotic nuclear reactions. Electrons start to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378000,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be driven into the iron nuclei in a process called electron capture. The negatively charged electrons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "merge with positively charged protons to \nproduce neutrons. In this way, Iron is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390560,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "converted into elements with fewer protons, but which are still just as heavy as iron and very neutron rich."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398000,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once we’ve gone down a few hundred meters, we see nuclei that can’t even exist outside  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a neutron star. Where the star is 50 billion \ntimes the density of earth, we might find a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409280,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleus like Zinc-80, which would decay in \nhalf a second on earth by ejecting neutrons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nuclei with such high ratios of neutrons to \nprotons are only stabilized by the incredible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422320,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressures and extreme electron \nenergies in the neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426320,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we leave the outer crust for the inner crust, our nuclei become so neutron-rich that they start  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fall apart. We call this “neutron drip” - \nneutrons leak from nuclei into the ever-narrowing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439360,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space between them. Now we don’t know exactly how deep this phenomenon begins, but our best calculations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 445040,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suggest it’s close to half a kilometer deep, and we’re at least a trillion times the density of matter  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Earth. We are really relying on our theoretical calculations here - we’re beyond the point where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can duplicate these energies and these \nneutron-rich nuclei in particle accelerators."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463440,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the neutron drip intensifies, the \nspace between the nuclei fills with a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467520,
    "dDurationMs": 6646,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutron gas. Meanwhile the electron gas gets thinner due to the electron capture process.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474166,
    "dDurationMs": 6474,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the neutron gas starts to take over the role of the electrons. Neutrons are also fermions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480640,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so two of them can’t occupy the same state. The star is now increasingly supported by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485440,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutron degeneracy pressure. But neutrons \ncan get much closer to each other before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490240,
    "dDurationMs": 5337,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this degeneracy pressure kicks in, and so \nmuch, much higher densities become possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495577,
    "dDurationMs": 4503,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Further down the nuclei themselves \nstart to get fuzzy as protons are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outnumbered by neutrons 5 to 1. A given \nneutron’s wavefunction is so spread out  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505600,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that it becomes hard to even localize \nit to being inside a given nucleus.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511280,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the time we reach the bottom of the crust, around a kilometer deep, densities have reached  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516240,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "100 trillion times that of the Earth. Here, the once-distinct nuclei are beginning to touch each other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we drop beneath the inner core, things \nfinally start to get properly weird.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528800,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Welcome to the pasta. Specifically,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532400,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear pasta. This is perhaps the least known and most freaky state of matter in the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 538560,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When nuclei start to touch they rearrange, forming exotic shapes. Nuclei have a sort of competition-  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while neutrons and protons feel a very strong short range attraction to each other due to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strong nuclear force, the electric repulsion between the remaining protons tries to push them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556400,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as far away from each other as they can. Down here there may be 20 neutrons for each proton,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562320,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so the protons can’t really resist the forces reshaping the nuclei. The result is this game  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of particle tug-of-war, with all its pushing and pulling, we see a complete rearrangement of matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574720,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nuclei reform radically, forming cylinders \ncontaining many millions of protons and neutrons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582240,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nuclear physicists affectionately call this phase \nof matter spaghetti. At slightly higher densities,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588800,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this nuclear spaghetti may be squeezed together to form sheets. That’s right - nuclear lasagna. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And because it’s so dense, it’s really \nhard to bend and move this stuff. Nuclear pasta  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600960,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might even be the strongest material in the \nuniverse, a quintillion times stronger than steel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The enormous strength of nuclear pasta allows it to resist the insane gravitational forces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so support a sort of jumbled texture - sort of like nuclear pasta mountains buried beneath  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the star’s surface. These could be as tall as \n10 centimeters. which doesn’t sound like a lot,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623440,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until you remember that every cubic centimeter of nuclear pasta weighs as much as a mountain on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628720,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth. As the neutron star rotates, these buried neutron star mountain ranges get dragged in circles,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635360,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "making a very weak gravitational wave \nsignal. These gravitational waves are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639840,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much weaker than the signals we’ve detected when neutron stars or black holes merge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644240,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so it’s much harder to detect them. But instead of being a big splash and a crash, it’s a continuous  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651120,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hum at exactly one frequency- twice the \nfrequency of the neutron star’s rotation-  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656480,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and gravitational wave astronomers are searching for these signals with LIGO right now, targeting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulsars in our galaxy using their known rotation frequencies. They haven’t found anything yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669040,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but they might soon, giving us a first glimpse at the inner workings of neutron stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674160,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the time we reach the bottom of the pasta layer, just above the neutron star core,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all that matter has been smooshed together into a soup of mostly neutrons and just the occasional proton.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685200,
    "dDurationMs": 4458,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The density is here is 200 trillion \ntimes anything found on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689658,
    "dDurationMs": 5302,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "​​And so we’ve arrived at the core, where we find the most extreme conditions in the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694960,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "entire modern universe. Here, pairs \nof spin-½ neutrons become connected  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700880,
    "dDurationMs": 8984,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a particular way to form Cooper pairs, \nwhich act as spin-0 or spin-1 particles.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709864,
    "dDurationMs": 5816,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of our fermions effectively become bosons - which means they can do some pretty crazy stuff.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715680,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of neutrons they can become \na superfluid - a frictionless fluid that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can do things like sustain vortices with enormous amounts of energy. Some physicists think that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725840,
    "dDurationMs": 7730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dissipation of these vortices is seen by us as glitches in the frequency in the flashes of pulsars.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733570,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rare cooper-pair protons on the other hand turn the core into a superconductor,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 738880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is probably an essential part of maintaining the neutron star’s enormous magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743840,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Approaching the dead center of the \nneutron star and even the protons and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747760,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrons start to lose structure and mush \ntogether. This is all highly theoretical,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752240,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it may be that these extreme pressures and energies we find ‘hyperon’ particles containing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "‘strange quarks’. Or, they might not be bound into particles at all - the protons and neutrons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763920,
    "dDurationMs": 6639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "may dissolve completely into a quark gluon plasma. And we've talked about quark stars before. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 770559,
    "dDurationMs": 3418,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While these plasmas have been seen in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773977,
    "dDurationMs": 6263,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collider experiments on Earth, we’re not sure if they exist in neutron stars. The only other time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter existed naturally in conditions like these was within a fraction of a second of the big bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786320,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you're quite for second, listen carefully you might hear the faint \nbooms of thermonuclear storms raging above us on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794240,
    "dDurationMs": 7239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the surface. It seems our neutron star has started accreting matter from a binary partner star.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801479,
    "dDurationMs": 6521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its mass is growing and at some point soon it’ll form an inescapable event horizon and we’ll be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808000,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stuck inside an actual black hole. Escape will become even more impossible than it already is.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 814720,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think it’s time to power \nup our anti-gravity boosters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818080,
    "dDurationMs": 6633,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and leave behind the strangest and most \nextreme parts in all of space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
